WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.900
Element 105, Dubnium,...

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.600
...was the shortest video we made,
 at 45 seconds.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:10.580
I know very little about Dubnium. 
I'm not sure anybody knows much about it.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:15.280
And I realized there was a lot more to say about it
 than we did the first time.

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:16.720
And in particular,...

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:18.760
...I have a really nice sample,...

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:20.380
...which is relevant,...

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.060
...which I'll tell you about in a minute.

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:27.040
No, this is not a sample of Dubnium.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:29.240
But...   Dubnium, like...

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:32.600
...all the elements
 in this part of the periodic table,...

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:34.740
...can only be made synthetically,...

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:37.560
...by accelerating a light atom...

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.360
...into a heavier one, and they fuse together.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:43.360
In this case, the light atom is Neon,...

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:48.520
...which has atomic number 10,
 and the heavy atom is Americium,...

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:51.100
...atomic number 95.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:54.660
10 plus 95 makes 105.

00:00:54.660 --> 00:00:59.640
One of the reasons
 that Dubnium is so important, in my view,...

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.100
...is because...

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:03.420
...there was a huge argument...

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.900
...between the first experiments,...

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:10.520
...when it was first synthesized,
 which were in the early 1970s,...

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:13.340
...and the time when it was finally named,...

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:16.380
...which was in the late 1990s.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:20.620
And really, it was Dubnium and Seaborgium,...

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:23.840
...and the arguments
 about the naming of these elements,...

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:25.840
...that really sorted out...

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:27.820
...the really amicable way...

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:31.300
...that we now have
 of considering the discovery...

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:33.680
...of these new super-heavy elements.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:35.700
The original experiments...

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.540
...were done in a place called Dubna,...

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:40.720
...which Brady and I have visited,...

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:45.820
...where the Joint Institute for Nuclear Research
 is located.

00:01:45.820 --> 00:01:50.300
This is a big center
 for accelerators in Russia.

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:55.260
And you need accelerators
 to accelerate up the light ions...

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:57.980
...to smash into the heavy target.

00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:01.380
And... the difficulty is,
 you only make these elements...

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.320
...one atom at a time.

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:08.340
So it isn't as if you have
 a large lump of the material to analyze.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:10.880
The other problem is,
 they're very short-lived,...

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:13.560
...anything from seconds to a few hours.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:17.860
So you can't accumulate a lot of atoms.
  The way you detect them...

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:20.800
...is that...
 you detect their decay.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:22.960
They give out gamma rays,...

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:26.120
...and they also form daughter compounds,...

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:28.040
...which themselves decay.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:31.040
And you can predict with some accuracy...

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:35.020
...what energy of gamma rays you would expect,...

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:39.780
...and then, quite quickly, 
you get to known radioactive elements,...

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:41.360
...so you can work backwards.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:43.680
The problem is that,...

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.640
...if you want to synthesize...

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:49.520
...really, or as stable an atom as possible,...

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:55.140
...you need to have as many neutrons as possible
 in your combined atom.

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:59.040
The option for making
 Dubnium, element 105,...

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:02.540
...with a large excess of neutrons,
 is not very great.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:07.580
So they never managed to synthesize
 any very stable isotopes.

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:10.840
The difficulty came that a rival group...

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:12.720
...in America, in Berkeley,...

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.620
...discovered the element at the same time.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:18.360
And there was a huge argument of priority.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:19.920
We discovered it first!

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:22.740
There was an interesting meeting...

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:25.260
...between Seaborg...

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:26.020
...and...

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:28.820
...Flyorov, the leader of the Russian team,...

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:31.380
...and they tried to resolve this argument.

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:33.260
But it was unsuccessful,...

00:03:33.260 --> 00:03:35.940
...and this led to the idea,...

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:38.380
...after some considerable argument,...

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:41.600
...of forming a committee
 that would look...

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:43.500
...at the...

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:45.780
...evidence from the different teams,...

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:48.940
...an independent committee, and decide...

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:52.860
...who should take the priority
 for a particular discovery.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:58.220
And in the case of Dubnium, it was decided
 the Berkeley and Dubna teams...

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:01.700
...had discovered it
 more or less at the same time.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:03.440
Then why was it named after Dubna?

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:08.260
I think there was then a friendly agreement
 that it should be named after Dubna,...

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:09.900
...not least because...

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:13.900
...Berkeley already had
 3 elements named in its own name,..

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:16.900
...Californium, Americium, and Berkelium.

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:18.260
Plus Seaborgium, Professor.

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:20.500
Seaborgium was named...

00:04:20.500 --> 00:04:22.720
...slightly later, but at the same time.

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:25.140
Was there a bit of horse trading
 going on, do you think?

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:28.120
Like, we'll give you an element
 if you give us an element?

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:33.480
I suspect.   But it was also complicated, 
because when they finally made the decision,...

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:37.580
...they also named the elements
 that had been discovered in Darmstadt,...

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:40.160
...Meitnerium, Hassium, and so on.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:43.320
So they were all named more or less simultaneously

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:46.440
The thing that I think is really remarkable...

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:51.720
...is that, although they have made
 relatively few atoms of Dubnium,...

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:55.400
...they managed to do
 a whole series of experiments...

00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:58.440
...that can tell you
 quite a lot about the chemistry.

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:03.900
Dubnium comes immediately
 underneath Tantalum, in the periodic table.

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:09.980
So you would expect the chemistry of Dubnium
 to be really quite like Tantalum,...

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:13.480
...but it turns out,
 when they did experiments,...

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:16.600
that it was perhaps
 slightly more like Niobium,...

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:21.100
...which is the element
 immediately above Tantalum in that group.

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:24.980
These are experiments
 that are just done with a few atoms.

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:29.100
They're looking at the formation
 of different sorts of compounds,...

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:34.560
...and how these compounds,
 for example, stick to a piece of glass,...

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:36.440
...or don't stick to a piece of glass,...

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:41.700
...or whether, if you precipitate them,
say with a niobium compound,...

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:46.220
...does the radioactivity precipitate,
 or does it stay in solution?

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:49.200
The reason why these things are interesting...

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:50.600
...is because,...

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:53.460
...as the atoms get bigger and heavier,...

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:59.240
...the relativistic effects caused
 by the electrons traveling at very high speeds...

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:01.280
...lead to the possibility...

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:06.080
...that the periodic table,
 the periodic properties, may break down.

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:08.800
So it's really quite important that Dubnium...

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:13.260
...shows the properties
 of its own group in the periodic table,...

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:17.520
...which shows, at least, that at element 105...

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:21.220
...the periodic properties still behave...

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:23.240
...reasonably as one would expect.

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:26.480
This sample, here, is a piece of granite,...

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:28.800
...sort of red Russian granite,...

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:32.240
...which I picked up
 on the bank of the river Volga,...

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:33.840
...which flows...

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:34.740
...through...

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:40.760
...the city of Dubna, past the Institute,
 when Brady and I went there.

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:45.800
So, I got my piece of granite
 and my Dubna mug,...

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:48.160
...which I use for drinking from.

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:49.520
I think...

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:52.280
...the really important lesson...

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:53.960
...from Dubnium...

00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:57.340
...is, that when it was first synthesized,...

00:06:57.340 --> 00:07:01.680
...there was huge tension
 between the different groups around the world...

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:03.800
...that were making these elements.

00:07:03.800 --> 00:07:06.160
But now, it's realized...

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:10.680
...that as we go further and further
 along the periodic table,...

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:13.140
...making heavier and heavier elements,...

00:07:13.140 --> 00:07:17.400
...the only way
 that we're going to succeed as scientists...

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:22.860
...is if everybody works together
 in a collaborative and friendly way.

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:26.900
So I think you should see Dubnium as...

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:33.560
...the start of this collaboration, which now,
 as Brady and I have seen, is really warm...

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:36.920
...between the various groups involved

00:07:43.660 --> 00:07:45.680
Thank you so much
 for watching this video,...

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:47.860
...and down in the video description 
I'll put links...

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:52.220
...from our trip to Dubna, along with other videos
 about super-heavy elements,...

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:55.720
...and also about...
 not so heavy elements.

00:07:55.900 --> 00:07:59.540
And if you're looking for something different, 
why not check out my channel, "OBJECTIVITY",...

00:07:59.540 --> 00:08:03.860
...where you can watch videos
 about historic scientific objects, like this,...

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:05.320
...and this,...

00:08:05.320 --> 00:08:07.120
...and this as well,...

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:11.240
...and, more recently, this video
 about the history of driverless cars,...

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:12.240
...and...

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:13.440
...well,...

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:16.680
...I'll let you find out about this thing for yourselves.

